WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.305
Lesson 3.6.

00:00:02.305 --> 00:00:05.115
Lending design deep dive: e-mode, caps,

00:00:05.115 --> 00:00:08.015
auctions, soft liquidation, bad debt.

00:00:08.015 --> 00:00:09.939
By the end of this lesson, you'll

00:00:09.939 --> 00:00:12.033
be able to read a lending protocol's

00:00:12.033 --> 00:00:14.070
risk parameters like a risk manager.

00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:16.660
Here's the plan.

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:18.345
First, the key ideas.

00:00:18.345 --> 00:00:21.060
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:22.585
Then, your checklist.

00:00:22.585 --> 00:00:24.747
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:26.047 --> 00:00:32.037
First, Efficiency mode (e-mode): higher LTVs for correlated assets

00:00:32.037 --> 00:00:37.702
(e.g. an LST against ETH, or stablecoin against stablecoin).

00:00:37.702 --> 00:00:40.273
Great for correlated loops; dangerous if

00:00:40.273 --> 00:00:42.642
the correlation breaks (a depeg).

00:00:42.642 --> 00:00:46.164
Next, Isolation mode / isolated markets: newer or

00:00:46.164 --> 00:00:50.039
riskier collateral can only back limited borrowing, so

00:00:50.039 --> 00:00:53.157
its failure can't infect the whole pool.

00:00:53.157 --> 00:00:56.051
Then, Supply and borrow caps: limits on how

00:00:56.051 --> 00:00:58.485
much of an asset can be deposited or

00:00:58.485 --> 00:01:03.127
borrowed, protecting against manipulation and illiquid collateral.

00:01:03.127 --> 00:01:07.655
Also, Liquidation mechanisms: a fixed bonus (liquidators get e.g.

00:01:07.655 --> 00:01:11.153
5% extra) vs Dutch auctions (the price falls until

00:01:11.153 --> 00:01:14.926
someone buys, a better price discovery in calm markets

00:01:14.926 --> 00:01:19.317
but vulnerable if keepers fail) vs soft liquidation (collateral

00:01:19.317 --> 00:01:23.090
is converted gradually across price bands as the price

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:26.588
falls, and can convert back if the price recovers;

00:01:26.588 --> 00:01:30.867
losses come from the conversions rather than one penalty).

00:01:30.867 --> 00:01:33.241
On top of that, Bad debt: when collateral

00:01:33.241 --> 00:01:35.632
is worth less than the debt it backs.

00:01:35.632 --> 00:01:38.562
Who pays: a reserve or insurance fund, a

00:01:38.562 --> 00:01:42.707
staking "safety module", or lenders pro rata (socialised).

00:01:44.007 --> 00:01:46.821
And finally, Risk curators / risk managers

00:01:46.821 --> 00:01:49.897
set these parameters; governance approves them.

00:01:51.197 --> 00:01:54.507
Let's look at the diagram: The liquidation cascade.

00:01:54.507 --> 00:01:56.067
Take a moment with it.

00:01:56.067 --> 00:01:58.477
It's everything we've just said, in one picture.

00:01:59.777 --> 00:02:02.292
Let's work an example, step by step.

00:02:02.292 --> 00:02:06.210
An LST loop in e-mode at 90% LTV

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:11.852
looks safe because LST and ETH move together.

00:02:11.852 --> 00:02:14.680
If the LST trades at a 6% discount on

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:18.921
the oracle during a panic, the "correlated" position can

00:02:18.921 --> 00:02:22.942
be liquidated even though ETH's price didn't move.

00:02:22.942 --> 00:02:26.208
Check what oracle prices the LST (market

00:02:26.208 --> 00:02:29.952
price vs exchange rate) before trusting e-mode.

00:02:31.252 --> 00:02:32.712
Here's your checklist.

00:02:32.712 --> 00:02:34.247
Do it now, for real.

00:02:34.247 --> 00:02:36.579
First, for each market: LTV,

00:02:36.579 --> 00:02:41.142
liquidation threshold, bonus/mechanism, caps, oracle.

00:02:41.142 --> 00:02:43.480
Next, I know who absorbs bad debt

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:45.637
in each protocol I lend to.

00:02:45.637 --> 00:02:47.991
And finally, E-mode positions have a

00:02:47.991 --> 00:02:50.091
depeg scenario in my stress test.

00:02:51.391 --> 00:02:52.601
Question 1.

00:02:52.601 --> 00:02:53.726
What is e-mode for?

00:02:58.146 --> 00:03:01.495
The answer: Higher LTVs on correlated assets.

00:03:02.795 --> 00:03:04.040
Question 2.

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:07.835
How does soft liquidation differ from a fixed-bonus liquidation?

00:03:12.255 --> 00:03:15.950
The answer: Collateral is converted gradually across price

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:18.831
bands (and can convert back) instead of being

00:03:18.831 --> 00:03:20.835
seized in one go with a penalty.

00:03:22.135 --> 00:03:23.405
Question 3.

00:03:23.405 --> 00:03:25.280
Who can end up paying for bad debt?

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:32.394
The answer: Protocol reserves, a safety

00:03:32.394 --> 00:03:34.280
module, or lenders pro rata.

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:36.825
Let's recap.

00:03:36.825 --> 00:03:41.373
Efficiency mode (e-mode): higher LTVs for correlated assets (e.g.

00:03:41.373 --> 00:03:45.395
an LST against ETH. Isolation mode / isolated markets.

00:03:45.395 --> 00:03:46.821
Supply and borrow caps: limits on how much

00:03:46.821 --> 00:03:48.480
of an asset can be deposited or borrowed.

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:54.420
Liquidation mechanisms: a fixed bonus (liquidators get e.g.

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:58.859
5% extra) vs Dutch auctions (the price falls until

00:03:58.859 --> 00:04:03.646
someone buys, a better price discovery in calm markets

00:04:03.646 --> 00:04:09.217
but vulnerable if keepers fail) vs soft liquidation (collateral

00:04:09.217 --> 00:04:14.004
is converted gradually across price bands as the price

00:04:14.004 --> 00:04:18.356
falls, and can convert back if the price recovers.

00:04:19.656 --> 00:04:22.171
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:04:22.171 --> 00:04:24.455
Next up, Lesson 3.7: CDP

00:04:24.455 --> 00:04:27.926
stablecoins: minting your own dollars.
